# Global HTTP Loading Indicator: Debounced, Flicker-Free, and Poll-Safe

## 1. What is it?

A **global HTTP loading indicator** is a single top-of-page progress bar (like the one on YouTube or GitHub) that shows whenever the app is talking to the backend, instead of every component wiring up its own local "loading" spinner.

The interesting part isn't "show a bar when `HttpClient` is busy" — that's a one-line interceptor. The interesting part is making it feel *good*:

- it shouldn't flash on for 50ms and vanish (flicker) on fast requests
- it shouldn't stay stuck on screen because of a slow background poll that nobody is actually waiting on

So the real feature is a **debounced, request-counting, selectively-scoped loading signal**, driven by an `HttpInterceptorFn` and exposed to the UI as a plain signal.

## 2. Why was it introduced?

Before a shared solution like this, teams usually end up in one of two bad places:

- **Every component manages its own `isLoading` flag** — `component.isLoading = true` before the call, `false` in `finalize()`. This duplicates the same boilerplate everywhere, and nobody remembers to handle the "component destroyed mid-request" case consistently.
- **A single naive global flag**, flipped directly by an interceptor: `loading = true` on request, `loading = false` on response. This looks fine until you actually use it:
  - Two buttons trigger two parallel requests. The first one finishes and naively sets `loading = false`, hiding the bar while the second request is still in flight.
  - A request that resolves in 40ms still causes a visible flash of the bar for one render frame — distracting, feels buggy, users notice it more than a smooth "no bar at all" would have.
  - A background poll (dashboard refresh every 5s, notification check, etc.) is technically "HTTP activity" too. If it's tracked the same way as user-initiated calls, the bar can end up permanently on screen, or flicker every poll cycle, even though the user isn't waiting on it at all.

So the actual problems to solve are: **counting** (don't hide while other requests are still pending), **debouncing** (don't show for very short requests, and once shown don't hide the instant it's technically done), and **scoping** (let some requests opt out of "global loading" entirely).

## 3. How does it work?

**Request counting.** A `LoadingService` keeps an internal `activeRequests` counter, not a boolean. `show()` increments it; `hide()` decrements it. The *visible* state only flips when the counter crosses the 0 ↔ 1 boundary — so five overlapping requests don't hide the bar until the very last one finishes.

**Debounce with a "show delay" and a "minimum visible time".** The raw true/false stream is piped through `switchMap`:

- when it becomes `true`, wait `SHOW_DELAY_MS` (e.g. 150ms) before actually emitting `true`. If the request finishes before that timer fires, `switchMap` cancels the pending "show" timer — the bar never appears. This kills the flicker on fast requests.
- when it becomes `false`, wait `MIN_VISIBLE_MS` (e.g. 400ms) before emitting `false`. This guarantees that once the bar *is* visible, it stays up long enough to read, instead of blinking off after one frame.

**Scoping via `HttpContextToken`.** Not every HTTP call represents something the user is waiting on. Angular's `HttpContext` lets you attach metadata to a single request that interceptors can read. A `SKIP_LOADING` token, defaulting to `false`, lets any call opt out:

```ts
this.http.get('/api/poll', { context: new HttpContext().set(SKIP_LOADING, true) });
```

The loading interceptor checks that flag first and, if set, just forwards the request without ever calling `show()`/`hide()` — so a slow background poll can literally never affect the bar, no matter how long it takes. This is the cleanest fix for "one slow poll shouldn't keep the bar spinning forever": don't count it in the first place, rather than trying to cap a shared timer after the fact.

**Exposing it to templates.** The interceptor/service logic is naturally RxJS (interceptors return/consume `Observable`s). The final state is converted to a signal once, at the boundary, with `toSignal()` — so every component just reads `loadingService.loading()` and renders with `@if`, with no manual subscription management anywhere in the UI layer.

## 4. Example

Standalone Angular demo: a shared `LoadingBarComponent` reads the debounced signal; `AppComponent` has "Quick request" (200ms) and "Slow request" (2000ms) buttons that go through the bar, plus a background poll running every 5s (each call simulated at 6s) that's deliberately excluded via `SKIP_LOADING`.

